## Lore

The Merchant Trader, or Outback Trader, lives by buying cheap and trading up.
He deals in whatever turns a profit: furs, water, fuel, batteries, soap, cloth,
computers, medicine, herbs, building materials, vehicles, armor, weapons,
ammunition, tools and information. Many also act as the business agent of an
adventuring or mercenary group, negotiating the price and terms of its work.

Most are friendly, well dressed, well educated and persuasive, and most would
much rather talk than fight or labor. Some are honest, some sell snake oil and
some are plain crooks, but together they are a lifeline between Outback
communities, carrying goods, news and contacts from one settlement to the
next. They tend to know many people and very little about surviving in the
wild.

## GM Notes

A group''s fee, as the Trader usually negotiates it: about 25% cash, 50-60%
trade goods, and 15-25% services and expenses (healing, room and board, a
guide, use of a vehicle, discounts or favors). About 75% of Merchant Traders
hate getting personally involved in physical labor or fighting.

Special Trader abilities the sheet does not roll for you: Contacts is 15% +4%
per level, +4% per every five M.A. points above 18. Spotting a fake with
Gemology is at -10%.

Starting kit beyond what the sheet lists: a pair of scissors, a wristwatch, a
mini-pocket fan, a suitcase, satchel or briefcase with 10 M.D.C., personal
items, one suit of excellent quality for meetings and formal occasions, and a
collection of cheap give-aways to impress clients (gum, candy, fruit,
moonshine or beer, cigarettes, refillable pens, markers, beads). He starts
with a modest vehicle, typically a jeep, pickup truck or hover car.

Money: besides the cash, 2D6x$1000 in saleable or tradeable stock, anything
from food, seeds or skins to electronics, fuel, M.D. weapons, clothing or
medicine. An NPC has twice as much, and is likely to have 2-8 assistants.

Supply and demand (printed 70): Perth and Melbourne want grain, metal ores,
seedlings, meat and fossil fuels; Outbackers want nearly every modern machine
and convenience, above all electronics, medicine, ammunition, M.D. weapons and
armor, vehicles and processed fuel; Outlanders want fuel, medicine, processed
food and machine parts; Aboriginal tribes and Reef Islanders do not trade.
